                <pb facs="0588" n="468" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">723.2515/945: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Peru</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sterling</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-05-25T20:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 25, 1922—8
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 11 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>49. As reported in my 48, May 24, 2 p.m.,<anchor n="9" xml:id="d218-f1"/> I had
                    an interview yesterday with the President, and this afternoon I saw the Minister
                    of Foreign Affairs, who stated that it was decided at a meeting of the Council
                    of Ministers and of the Diplomatic Committee of the Congress that the Government
                    of Peru accepts the views of the Department of State. The delegates to the
                    conference will confine themselves to the unfulfilled portions of the treaty and
                    will not be instructed to present the claim that the treaty has been nullified,
                    but are to contend that article 3 has been violated and cannot now be fulfilled.
                    It would seem from our conversation that the Minister expects disagreement with
                    Chile over this article to precipitate arbitration and that all claims that
                    Chile has violated the treaty will thereupon be brought up.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sterling</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
